GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:170: 	    walked access FILLED THE TRANSLATION FROM the page's first bytes.  ("every
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:171: 	    walked access resolved to" until 2026-09-07: a pass-2 seat noted that the
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:172: 	    Alpha FAST PATH recomputes `addr & 8191' itself, so a later cache HIT reads
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:173: 	    the right bytes.  The corruption is the SLOW-PATH FILL, which is also why a
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:174: 	    watchpoint -- which keeps a page off the fast store table -- makes it
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:175: 	    reproducible.)  Measured: a cold guest ldq
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:176: 	    of vaddr+8 returned the vaddr+0 value.  A debugger watchpoint on a walked page
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:177: 	    landed at the base for the same reason.
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:178: 	
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:179: 	    The mask is derived from pageSizeShift rather than written as 0x1fff: this
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:180: 	    function already has TWO names for 13 (ALPHA_PAGESHIFT and this local), and a
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:181: 	    literal beside the shift is how the two drift.  `|' rather than `+' because
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:182: 	    that is what every other CPU's walk in this tree writes; here they are equal,
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:183: 	    the shifted PFN having bits 12:0 clear.  The TODO below STAYS: `flags' is
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:184: 	    still ignored, the protection bits untested, and `return 2' still reports
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:185: 	    every walked page writable.  */
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:186: 	(*return_paddr) = (ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift)
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:187: 	    | (vaddr & ((1ULL << pageSizeShift) - 1));
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:188: 
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_alpha.c:189: 	// TODO.
