GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:136:  *  host pointer for THAT page.  memory_paddr_to_hostaddr() returns a pointer into a 1 MB
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:137:  *  memblock, so the copy walks host memory that is contiguous PHYSICALLY while the guest's
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:138:  *  span is contiguous VIRTUALLY.  Those coincide only by luck, and the moment the length
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:139:  *  carries past the page boundary the guest reads -- or OVERWRITES -- whatever physical
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:140:  *  page happens to follow.  Measured offline against a deliberately non-monotonic map: a
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:141:  *  straddling read returned the physically-following page's bytes, and a straddling WRITE
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:142:  *  left the guest's intended page untouched while corrupting an unrelated one.  Measured
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:143:  *  too: when the tail page had no valid translation the old code returned
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:144:  *  MEMORY_ACCESS_OK anyway, so this was a MISSING FAULT as well as a wrong page.
