GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:171:  *  performs the sibling-call optimisation at -O2 and -O3, leaving a 96-byte span.  So the
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:172:  *  loop is still the right implementation (it does not depend on an optimisation being
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:173:  *  applied, and -O0 builds and other compilers are real), but the justification was wrong,
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:174:  *  and the row that certified it was VACUOUS until the differential was given
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:175:  *  -fno-optimize-sibling-calls.  No caller in the tree passes an access large enough for
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:176:  *  this to bite today; it is insurance, and it costs about fifteen lines.
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:177:  *
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:178:  *  ONLY WHEN A TRANSLATION ACTUALLY HAPPENS.  Under PHYSICAL (or with no translate_v2p)
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:179:  *  paddr == vaddr, there is no per-page mapping to redo, and splitting would buy nothing
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:180:  *  while turning one large DMA into thousands of calls -- so those go straight through and
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:181:  *  the memblock split keeps doing its job unchanged.
GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:182:  */
